    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Transparency International (TI) Pakistan, though long-term advocacy and collaboration with local civil society organisations and provincial government, successfully pushed for the creation of the Balochistan Information Commission &#x2013; an institution mandated under the 2021 Balochistan Right to Information Act but never implemented. With Australian support through the Transparency International Indo-Pacific Partnership (TI IPP) Program Phase 2, the chapter engaged key political stakeholders, including the Chief Minister, to uphold the legal requirement that the Commission be established within four months of the Act&#x2019;s passage. 
&#x200d;
In November 2024, the Balochistan Provincial Government formally announced the Commission&#x2019;s creation. This major milestone will help ensure that public institutions in Balochistan are legally required to disclose key data such as budgets, decision-making processes and official entitlements. The establishment of the Commission marks a significant advancement for transparency, citizen empowerment and anti-corruption in the province. These achievements in Balochistan highlight how the TI IPP Program Phase 2, DFAT&#x2019;s principal regional anti-corruption partnership, is supporting governments and civic actors to translate transparency laws into practice.</narrative>
    </description>
