    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Under the Transparency International Indo-Pacific Partnership (TI IPP) Program Phase 2, Australia supported TI&#x2019;s Pakistan Chapter to help embed robust anti-corruption measures in Pakistan&#x2019;s $7 billion Extended Fund Facility (EFF) agreement with the IMF, approved in September 2024. Through sustained advocacy, including direct engagement with the IMF and submission of policy recommendations in 2024, TI Pakistan successfully influenced the inclusion of governance and anti-corruption reforms as core conditions of the 37-month program. 
&#x200d;
Key outcomes included mandated administrative reforms focusing on fiscal transparency, strengthening governance and anti-corruption institutions, and transparency and accountability in public procurement. TI Pakistan&#x2019;s input, particularly on asset disclosure for public officials and corruption risk identification in high impact sectors, helped shape the structural benchmarks of the EFF. 
&#x200d;
These outcomes highlight how civil society, through evidence-based and inclusive engagement, can influence national stakeholders and shape international financing programs to align national reforms with global governance standards. TI Pakistan&#x2019;s efforts ensured anti-corruption is now an important pillar of Pakistan&#x2019;s path to macroeconomic stability. This work under the TI IPP Program Phase 2, DFAT&#x2019;s principal regional anti-corruption development investment, demonstrates how TI helps governments and civil society to integrate accountability into major economic reforms.</narrative>
    </description>
