    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">With support from Australia through the Transparency International Indo-Pacific Partnership (TI IPP) Program Phase 2, Transparency International (TI) Sri Lanka used the Right to Information (RTI) Act to improve equitable access to education. Investigations by the chapter&#x2019;s Advocacy and Legal Advice Centre (ALAC) uncovered widespread unlawful collection of school &#x2018;donations&#x2019; by government schools to fund curricular and extra-curricular activities. These practices placed unfair financial burdens on families and limited participation by low-income students. 
&#x200d;
Following public awareness-raising efforts and media engagement led by TI Sri Lanka&#x2019;s ALAC, the Ministry of Education acted decisively, issuing a ban on donation collection by schools. This outcome will help reduce financial barriers and enable all students, regardless of household income, to participate fully in school. It also demonstrated how civil society can bring about change, prompting other civil society organisations and individuals to use legal and advocacy tools, such as the RTI Act, to address grievances and promote social justice in Sri Lanka. The TI IPP Program Phase 2 is DFAT&#x2019;s principal regional investment supporting governments and civil society partners to address and prevent corruption in our region.</narrative>
    </description>
